[{:db/id 17592186129199 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-9bb2e441-17ac-5548-ad1a-c8ae9611922f" "question-b93ec44e-b9fb-53f6-875f-5ef36dc8b48a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186128853 :question/problem "Given that ![](images/14/14df7999d11afbe9002e05521d71f5008f2b49250238a714fada7dcc4aeb86d2.svg) express the following integral in polar coordinates:\n\n![](images/00/009865419103717553af1a73b3de0ac958a76eb2fdc9c95467236581d3ad0e11.svg)" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n![](images/82/829d32a5563a183ecc1f646b20fba584838dffb9ce67dcc0e014d2a90bcb7add.svg)\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region ![](images/f7/f7c8d853846850d1bf4afd67f7544f3f3c3756eb0de33ee5c27985e83449997d.svg)\n\n![](images/e4/e447e688e0f3e2c0411129e8d1baf4a900008173443f5392d98af52a27eb285b.png)\n\nNotice that the region ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg) represents the disk of radius ![](images/f5/f5f3224583db57b9cfe323c8489ef5e5271000002b5ea253eb98fd067a858b2d.svg) centered at ![](images/37/37cb790b6a640626cb093bf7ee969ddc9aafd1eb91c86d96a5339e6df2555fca.svg) \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n![](images/df/dfe1b368d21a4745d71b5fe084246ce0a16cb6a85ad983598442d0dbde0ed591.svg)\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ![](images/ae/ae3d9424cc536ab651dc8732546bd8bfea96901ce744793ec8797c02fc446ba4.svg) in polar coordinates:\n![](images/11/11d7065eda072cefb2c9ab63e679d3a0e7103a146a37b21440f0ff2e4f393369.svg) \n\nSince ![](images/3a/3a4ef1c1b261a53f9c45d0c90f2d44b155125fe1e7b1359406830111b4846f2f.svg) cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have ![](images/3f/3f7ce8399dec23cec965849d4623642dcaf947ca8500a837ce8ef771a42b7ee3.svg)\n\nNow, let's determine the limits for ![](images/3a/3a4ef1c1b261a53f9c45d0c90f2d44b155125fe1e7b1359406830111b4846f2f.svg) and ![](images/b9/b98533dee87a7bd8ed4d593a2aabd517621d6ab96e40a5b2ab4a8ac1923ddac5.svg) First of all, ![](images/3a/3a4ef1c1b261a53f9c45d0c90f2d44b155125fe1e7b1359406830111b4846f2f.svg) can vary from ![](images/63/632079982c24b29d4de5e322230ed61f106b1715de40fdb8b6879e982a9f3955.svg) (at the origin) to ![](images/4b/4b56a88cc6e7ba7c13d83cf18ea0f759a70af4d07186f2be1c558e25589e1c7c.svg) (at the boundary of the region): \n\n![](images/52/522df0e95e36cd67f34ffcc0ec4757c35749eab5d5137215c456c8b7e90994ae.png)\n\nSecondly, ![](images/06/06e47e2878930095abe73547809f3c9f87be3cd9b3b9069ca582970b60598840.svg) can vary between ![](images/64/64fd2741e70e8f1d2a64c58d05dc7124c6e6e94a5d7dea30124e4200e8c61e75.svg) (the negative ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-axis) and ![](images/b7/b76ea8adee01315895dd8c61949a5c8f28235929b53bbff59c58856e04ebd63f.svg) (the positive ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-axis):\n\n![](images/6a/6ad24b638877941296df25869f8b0ee6f9dc1e73a779a8a0da01de35d9f74f42.png)\n\nSo, in polar coordinates ![](images/49/4974a18e033f45691b0d3cab8bd7a16744004e4303f44184bb7045a7c64e6f8c.svg) the region can be expressed as \n![](images/9c/9cab9c4a9d446ee9fd9794a9455d46b63df1b53ce505236d7b91816cc6496890.svg)\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n![](images/5d/5d809200294cb2ddc88d35101eb6fc449902fce4ebb0b0fd9c883a32ee5c3b0c.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129198 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-1c4c9c61-2471-5bbd-bac4-3d2dcc4d6268" "question-9231aa24-53fe-5011-80b7-3b4b62ca8c09" "question-414bd17b-1eab-5df7-bb84-eb1080abae7b"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129200 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-2077302e-eca5-5aa1-8731-3c58ab648006"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-9bb2e441-17ac-5548-ad1a-c8ae9611922f" :question/id #uuid "9bb2e441-17ac-5548-ad1a-c8ae9611922f" :question/math-academy-id "q-83954" :question/problem "![](images/a6/a60d80425c240deb1ea372d7a21c3774247c4827536ce6f409f7e8736c7c94bc.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is the quarter-circle shown above. What is\n${∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $\\sqrt{3}$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le \\sqrt{3},\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d2c5fff5-18f1-537f-babe-36cf80cdf6a9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d2c5fff5-18f1-537f-babe-36cf80cdf6a9" :answer-field/id #uuid "d2c5fff5-18f1-537f-babe-36cf80cdf6a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e7a98739-7f64-53ee-a143-4b1433040ca0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-26c2bf0f-9ee2-5d88-8b23-545cf4448435" "answer-ccbd5dd6-3cb9-5c6a-a847-fed35c7db186" "answer-7c5313f3-0d47-59ad-80f4-3575ea706678" "answer-bdef2d30-346b-534b-81e2-deb246402953" "answer-e7a98739-7f64-53ee-a143-4b1433040ca0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-26c2bf0f-9ee2-5d88-8b23-545cf4448435" :answer/id #uuid "26c2bf0f-9ee2-5d88-8b23-545cf4448435" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ccbd5dd6-3cb9-5c6a-a847-fed35c7db186" :answer/id #uuid "ccbd5dd6-3cb9-5c6a-a847-fed35c7db186" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{3}}^{\\sqrt{3}}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7c5313f3-0d47-59ad-80f4-3575ea706678" :answer/id #uuid "7c5313f3-0d47-59ad-80f4-3575ea706678" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bdef2d30-346b-534b-81e2-deb246402953" :answer/id #uuid "bdef2d30-346b-534b-81e2-deb246402953" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{3}}^{\\sqrt{3}}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e7a98739-7f64-53ee-a143-4b1433040ca0" :answer/id #uuid "e7a98739-7f64-53ee-a143-4b1433040ca0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186389119 :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA={∬}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ represents the disk of radius $4$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 4,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\operatorname{ln}⁡((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+1)r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4}r\\operatorname{ln}⁡({r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+1)\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4}r\\operatorname{ln}⁡({r}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-b93ec44e-b9fb-53f6-875f-5ef36dc8b48a" :question/id #uuid "b93ec44e-b9fb-53f6-875f-5ef36dc8b48a" :question/math-academy-id "q-161043" :question/problem "![](images/2d/2db1e29e5f911a45c4c48ab41c4ad6dc63eeb36a8112672ae66844e14342f4c0.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is the circle shown above. What is ${∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A$ expressed in polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA={∬}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ represents the disk of radius $5$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 5,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}{(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{5}{r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e75c2f8e-ad0e-567f-ad60-4f9161bdb2d9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e75c2f8e-ad0e-567f-ad60-4f9161bdb2d9" :answer-field/id #uuid "e75c2f8e-ad0e-567f-ad60-4f9161bdb2d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-eb9c6068-17a0-54f3-9151-2a048c9f0abc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-37e9bbb5-54b5-5e26-a0a0-50d7cf20ec9d" "answer-6ef3239f-a18f-5059-bc4c-90c9f800c498" "answer-eb9c6068-17a0-54f3-9151-2a048c9f0abc" "answer-f55622cc-0b32-52f4-8ae8-9cf843cdb023" "answer-f3db7076-912a-5dca-b78b-50d779ed3d9a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-37e9bbb5-54b5-5e26-a0a0-50d7cf20ec9d" :answer/id #uuid "37e9bbb5-54b5-5e26-a0a0-50d7cf20ec9d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{25}{r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6ef3239f-a18f-5059-bc4c-90c9f800c498" :answer/id #uuid "6ef3239f-a18f-5059-bc4c-90c9f800c498" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{5}{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eb9c6068-17a0-54f3-9151-2a048c9f0abc" :answer/id #uuid "eb9c6068-17a0-54f3-9151-2a048c9f0abc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{5}{r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f55622cc-0b32-52f4-8ae8-9cf843cdb023" :answer/id #uuid "f55622cc-0b32-52f4-8ae8-9cf843cdb023" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{25}{r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f3db7076-912a-5dca-b78b-50d779ed3d9a" :answer/id #uuid "f3db7076-912a-5dca-b78b-50d779ed3d9a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{5}{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186278358 :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $4$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 4,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}{e}^{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4}r{e}^{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4}r{e}^{{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129627 :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $2$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2xy\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}2(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}2{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186278370 :question/problem "If $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4\\},$ then ${∬}_{D}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A=$" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA={∬}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/86/86036fd86096355b969da695f812c9bf835880edd68f7991183b70a6234e3eb4.png)\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents the disk of radius $2$ centered at $(2,0).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2)}^{2}+{(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits for $r$ and $\\theta .$ First of all, $r$ can vary from $r=0$ (at the origin) to $r=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ (at the boundary of the region): \n\n![](images/9e/9ef22dcc15175664331d989bd50d9a8d60a354a0780dbaf9465caca04f28c2d9.png)\n\nSecondly, $\\theta $ can vary between $\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ (the negative $y$-axis) and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ (the positive $y$-axis):\n\n![](images/f7/f724daf8321195815c9e413aab2106c611bc9d2e9653939266fc828aebeca68c.png)\n\nSo, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186278371 :question/problem "If $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,(x-4)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 16,\\,y\\le 0\\},$ then ${∬}_{D}{e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A=$" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA={∬}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/d8/d882bf70ecb7b94eee0eeeffbf63874ca8607268167f96278deb4678ec3b827a.png)\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $4$ centered at $(4,0).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $(x-4)^{2}+{y}^{2}=16$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =16 \\\\ {(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4)}^{2}+{(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2} & =16 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -8r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +16+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =16 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-8r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-8r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits for $r$ and $\\theta .$ First of all, $r$ can vary from $r=0$ (at the origin) to $r=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ (at the boundary of the region): \n\n![](images/60/605d9ce1c9030d1ac83c0a320bd648b42893f222279dd2d56b111acd9957c202.png)\n\nSecondly, $\\theta $ can vary between $\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ (the negative $y$-axis) and $\\theta =0$ (the positive $x$-axis):\n\n![](images/f3/f37e34042338af2b1ba5f0c214ebf046eeba068b0b3238b662910a868c069ee4.png)\n\nSo, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}{e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }r{e}^{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }r{e}^{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }r{e}^{{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-1c4c9c61-2471-5bbd-bac4-3d2dcc4d6268" :question/id #uuid "1c4c9c61-2471-5bbd-bac4-3d2dcc4d6268" :question/math-academy-id "q-83514" :question/problem "Evaluate \n${\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{2}2r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "First, we integrate with respect to $r,$ treating $\\theta $ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{2}2r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}[{r}^{2}]_{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}({2}^{2}-{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}(4-{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate with respect to $\\theta :$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}(4-{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =[4\\theta +\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ]_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))-(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})) \\\\  & =(2\\pi +0)-(\\pi +1) \\\\  & =\\pi -1\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4ca73348-922c-58d8-8087-d4874ab8f01d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4ca73348-922c-58d8-8087-d4874ab8f01d" :answer-field/id #uuid "4ca73348-922c-58d8-8087-d4874ab8f01d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0ea2ccdb-2b2b-5e06-a8bd-a29481af05f6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-77346f94-7bc7-5551-a9ff-5850737d6c7b" "answer-4e7ef1ee-27c1-50f3-af3d-a2b63e2d03d0" "answer-5e28ab03-180e-5b20-af37-557e7db4d91e" "answer-0ea2ccdb-2b2b-5e06-a8bd-a29481af05f6" "answer-5b3e76f1-42b1-5f3d-8b32-cf741baa2a0e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-77346f94-7bc7-5551-a9ff-5850737d6c7b" :answer/id #uuid "77346f94-7bc7-5551-a9ff-5850737d6c7b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4e7ef1ee-27c1-50f3-af3d-a2b63e2d03d0" :answer/id #uuid "4e7ef1ee-27c1-50f3-af3d-a2b63e2d03d0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi +1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5e28ab03-180e-5b20-af37-557e7db4d91e" :answer/id #uuid "5e28ab03-180e-5b20-af37-557e7db4d91e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi +2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0ea2ccdb-2b2b-5e06-a8bd-a29481af05f6" :answer/id #uuid "0ea2ccdb-2b2b-5e06-a8bd-a29481af05f6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi -1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5b3e76f1-42b1-5f3d-8b32-cf741baa2a0e" :answer/id #uuid "5b3e76f1-42b1-5f3d-8b32-cf741baa2a0e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi -2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-9231aa24-53fe-5011-80b7-3b4b62ca8c09" :question/id #uuid "9231aa24-53fe-5011-80b7-3b4b62ca8c09" :question/math-academy-id "q-83966" :question/problem "Evaluate \n${\\int }_{5\\pi /3}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "First, we integrate with respect to $r,$ treating $\\theta $ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{5\\pi /3}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & ={\\int }_{5\\pi /3}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{1}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{5\\pi /3}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,[\\frac{{r}^{2}}{2}]_{1}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{5\\pi /3}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ((2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}-{1}^{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{5\\pi /3}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -1)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{5\\pi /3}^{2\\pi }(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate with respect to $\\theta :$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{5\\pi /3}^{2\\pi }(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{2}{[-\\frac{4}{3}{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]}_{5\\pi /3}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[(-\\frac{4}{3}{cos}^{3}⁡(2\\pi )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi ))-(-\\frac{4}{3}{cos}^{3}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}))] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[(-\\frac{4}{3}+1)-(-\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{2})] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e9a63565-3b3e-5f4e-ac3a-4fbb44e6e16d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e9a63565-3b3e-5f4e-ac3a-4fbb44e6e16d" :answer-field/id #uuid "e9a63565-3b3e-5f4e-ac3a-4fbb44e6e16d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-566fc95d-9cf1-5eeb-ae90-c8f8a3969e01" :answer-field/choices ["answer-40fcf753-0267-53ac-aa07-33aca198c3c0" "answer-566fc95d-9cf1-5eeb-ae90-c8f8a3969e01" "answer-e43ffd51-6f41-504e-a3ea-d2e5f89becd2" "answer-686056c3-1712-5837-8d5c-57ad9de19f24" "answer-a84ffeca-f3ff-5817-85cf-b80c75007d36"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-40fcf753-0267-53ac-aa07-33aca198c3c0" :answer/id #uuid "40fcf753-0267-53ac-aa07-33aca198c3c0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{12}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-566fc95d-9cf1-5eeb-ae90-c8f8a3969e01" :answer/id #uuid "566fc95d-9cf1-5eeb-ae90-c8f8a3969e01" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e43ffd51-6f41-504e-a3ea-d2e5f89becd2" :answer/id #uuid "e43ffd51-6f41-504e-a3ea-d2e5f89becd2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-686056c3-1712-5837-8d5c-57ad9de19f24" :answer/id #uuid "686056c3-1712-5837-8d5c-57ad9de19f24" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a84ffeca-f3ff-5817-85cf-b80c75007d36" :answer/id #uuid "a84ffeca-f3ff-5817-85cf-b80c75007d36" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-414bd17b-1eab-5df7-bb84-eb1080abae7b" :question/id #uuid "414bd17b-1eab-5df7-bb84-eb1080abae7b" :question/math-academy-id "q-161048" :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}5{r}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta =$" :question/worked-solution "First, we integrate with respect to $r$ treating $\\theta $ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}5{r}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{1}5{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta [{r}^{5}]_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1-0)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate with respect to $\\theta :$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =-(-1-1) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4a4db397-2205-5620-8d4c-677784accd64"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4a4db397-2205-5620-8d4c-677784accd64" :answer-field/id #uuid "4a4db397-2205-5620-8d4c-677784accd64" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-73fa4118-327c-5da0-ace4-6324c8542c7b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5cf5461b-da0e-570c-b9aa-467a2f82071d" "answer-5d101e08-dd8a-5f3e-96a5-138bde512f04" "answer-73fa4118-327c-5da0-ace4-6324c8542c7b" "answer-e5117ec4-182c-58a0-848d-ff0ac8238d82" "answer-681f0aba-b493-51e6-a417-42799a6f3a67"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5cf5461b-da0e-570c-b9aa-467a2f82071d" :answer/id #uuid "5cf5461b-da0e-570c-b9aa-467a2f82071d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5d101e08-dd8a-5f3e-96a5-138bde512f04" :answer/id #uuid "5d101e08-dd8a-5f3e-96a5-138bde512f04" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-73fa4118-327c-5da0-ace4-6324c8542c7b" :answer/id #uuid "73fa4118-327c-5da0-ace4-6324c8542c7b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e5117ec4-182c-58a0-848d-ff0ac8238d82" :answer/id #uuid "e5117ec4-182c-58a0-848d-ff0ac8238d82" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-681f0aba-b493-51e6-a417-42799a6f3a67" :answer/id #uuid "681f0aba-b493-51e6-a417-42799a6f3a67" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186278363 :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/0a/0a1de805051eff4ea82b4eefb7bb113c724b3d4e7304704831b6294ee4e4d674.png)\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}4{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}4(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}4{r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{3}4{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,{[{r}^{4}]}_{0}^{3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta (81-0)\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =81{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =81{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{2}{[\\theta -\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta ]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{2}[(2\\pi -0)-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡0)] \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{2}\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\  & =81\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129887 :question/problem "![](images/fd/fd1a5a140c3a2d430312d6f6ceeb21d6b7282119c8ec53365553ff845d8c64d5.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral ${∬}_{D}x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A,$ where the region $D$ is the semicircle shown above." :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $\\frac{1}{2}$ centered at \n$(0,-\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $x=\\sqrt{-{y}^{2}-y}$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\sqrt{-{y}^{2}-y} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-{y}^{2}-y \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =-y \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} & =-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) & =-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2} & =-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}+r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nTherefore, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 0\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta [\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]_{0}^{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ((-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{4}-{0}^{4})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{4}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{[\\frac{{sin}^{5}⁡\\theta }{5}]}_{-\\pi /2}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}({sin}^{5}⁡0-{sin}^{5}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186128803 :answer-field/correct 17592186128804 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-2077302e-eca5-5aa1-8731-3c58ab648006" :question/id #uuid "2077302e-eca5-5aa1-8731-3c58ab648006" :question/math-academy-id "q-83520" :question/problem "Integrate the function $f(x,y)=x-y$ over the portion of the disk of radius $2$ centered at the origin, where $x\\ge 0$ and $y\\ge 0.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/7b/7b181b47af7d29b68d86d26b5c2b948c17ce39c01a62cbb36e874bb733865718.png)\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x-y\\,dA & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ){\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\frac{1}{3}{[{r}^{3}]}_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )({2}^{3}-{0}^{3})\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}[(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2})-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+\\operatorname{cos}⁡0)] \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}(1-1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0c8566ad-8855-54ed-9229-fd53a23bc1a3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0c8566ad-8855-54ed-9229-fd53a23bc1a3" :answer-field/id #uuid "0c8566ad-8855-54ed-9229-fd53a23bc1a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f171ab4d-9dad-5bbc-b321-f960560419b8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f171ab4d-9dad-5bbc-b321-f960560419b8" "answer-6f84c6cd-963a-5926-9438-431cae8fd173" "answer-3c68a910-d47a-5d42-8472-65a137268360" "answer-a1c001e9-e046-5dfd-859e-0f7553c95834" "answer-c1c797d0-94a3-5402-8500-48167fa5bf9a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f171ab4d-9dad-5bbc-b321-f960560419b8" :answer/id #uuid "f171ab4d-9dad-5bbc-b321-f960560419b8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6f84c6cd-963a-5926-9438-431cae8fd173" :answer/id #uuid "6f84c6cd-963a-5926-9438-431cae8fd173" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3c68a910-d47a-5d42-8472-65a137268360" :answer/id #uuid "3c68a910-d47a-5d42-8472-65a137268360" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{16}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a1c001e9-e046-5dfd-859e-0f7553c95834" :answer/id #uuid "a1c001e9-e046-5dfd-859e-0f7553c95834" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c1c797d0-94a3-5402-8500-48167fa5bf9a" :answer/id #uuid "c1c797d0-94a3-5402-8500-48167fa5bf9a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186354276 :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/e4/e4275e74a45deb22c0ed22546772e33fbd5caeb89a4036b1a2f4870e8ecf67a1.png)\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{2}{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+1}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2r}{{r}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡({r}^{2}+1)]_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2[\\theta ]_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}-\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi \\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129632 :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/b5/b5ba69ab02aa13e201b7b9113f6a36e705ad19e6412499504afc4f239459a8f3.png)\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(2x+y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{2}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ){\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,{[\\frac{{r}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )(\\frac{{2}^{3}}{3}-0)\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}[2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}((2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ))-(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}(1+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186130404 :tutorial/content "Evaluating double integrals by changing the variables to polar coordinates is often easier than using Cartesian coordinates.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{D}{{\\left( {x^2 + y^2} \\right)\\,\\mathrm d A}},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $D$ is the disk with center $(0,0)$ and radius $2,$ shown below.\n\n![](images/94/945f3881b5348fffb922d7b57fb42a6262eb03861fb5aa2411b947a16dcb3b59.png)\n\nThis is a challenging integral to evaluate as it currently stands. However, we should recognize that the problem naturally lends itself to plane polar coordinates because\n\n- the domain $D$ is a disc of radius $2$ centered at $O,$ which has a simple polar representation, and\n\n- the integrand $f(x,y) = x^2+y^2$ can also be expressed simply in polar coordinates.\n\nSo, let's transform the entire problem to polar coordinates using the transformation $\\mathbf T,$ defined by\n\n$$\nx=r\\cos\\theta, \\quad y=r\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nThe preimage of $D$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ is the rectangular region $\\Delta,$ given by \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\big\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 2, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq 2\\pi \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and its preimage $\\Delta$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/99/9901b20d218bef2935dba64612495874d08388dc6d7ae21f8a1de596296bc276.png)\n\n             \n\nTo express our integral in terms of polar coordinates, we will use the **change of variables formula,** given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\, {{\\color{black}{\\mathrm d A}}} = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r\\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  {{\\color{black}{r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta}}}.\n$$\n\nWhen expressing our integral in polar coordinates, we must write \n\n$$\n{\\color{black}{\\textrm d A}} = {\\color{black}{r\\,\\textrm d r\\textrm d \\theta}}.\n$$\n\nIt's important not to forget the extra factor of $r.$\n\nNow, applying the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,dA & ={∬}_{Δ}((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,dr\\,d\\theta  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,dr \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,dr \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot 4 \\\\  & =8\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{D}{{\\left( {x^2 + y^2} \\right)\\,\\mathrm d A}} = 8\\pi.\n$$\n\nIn terms of transformations, the change of variables formula maps the non-rectangular integration domain $D$ to the rectangular domain $\\Delta,$ making the integral much easier to evaluate." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186130406 :tutorial/content "Let's get a little more practice at writing regions in polar coordinates.\n\nConsider the semicircular disc of radius $1$ centered $(1,0)$ in the first quadrant, shown below. The equation of the curve is $y=\\sqrt{2x-x^2}.$\n\n![](images/0f/0f4aec039a0de31d8f5ea24dc9db8c83f236dfd66e9f181768dae45ab0fd6b07.png)\n\nFirst, we recall that the following equations relate Cartesian and polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now use the above formulas to write the function $y=\\sqrt{2x-x^2}$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{2x-{x}^{2}} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =2x-{x}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =2x \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} & =2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) & =2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2} & =2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}-2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r = 2\\cos\\theta.$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits for $r$ and $\\theta.$ First of all, $r$ can vary from $r=0$ (at the origin) to $r=2\\cos\\theta$ (at the boundary of the region): \n\n![](images/35/3584d2f0d9ee2c0ce469bfc117c67c1efd1f4fd08c96c7beef00198624977d06.png)\n\nSecondly, $\\theta$ can vary between $\\theta=0$ (the positive $x$-axis) and $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ (the positive $y$-axis):\n\n![](images/2c/2c5731478941621db03e8949eac217dcdfc604b3efd0312b56c59b9874d6be98.png)\n\nTherefore, in polar coordinates $(r, \\theta),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\Big\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 2\\cos\\theta, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\Big\\}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129371 :activity/expected-seconds 1080.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
